  

<h2 id="simple-or-not-what-works-for-others-might-be-a-distraction-in-disguise">Simple or not, what works for others might be a distraction in disguise</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/69b6785b-d4c7-47e5-8476-b1abeda7e02f/pasted-percent20image-percent2020261005132224.png" title="rough cartoon in black ink of someone striding purposefully down a path with handrails on either side. There's an alternate path with no handrails, and flowers, candies, and stars on the area off to the side of it." alt="rough cartoon in black ink of someone striding purposefully down a path with handrails on either side. There's an alternate path with no handrails, and flowers, candies, and stars on the area off to the side of it." width="2402" height="1758"></p>
<p>I’ve been using one big text file (<span class="small-caps">OBTF</span>) as a capture point and scratchpad for more than two years now. I love it! But this week I considered going back to using separate files for my daily notes. Again.</p>
<p>They look so cute, sitting there all in a row! And they’re baked into Obsidian, so what’s the problem? Lots of people have used daily notes for years and swear by them. And change is as good as a holiday, right?</p>
<p>So I set up a daily note workflow.</p>
<h3 id="daily-notes-are-one-of-the-funnest-malignancies-ive-ever-met">Daily notes are one of the funnest malignancies I’ve ever met</h3>
<p>Capture from all my devices via Drafts, check. A page in Obsidian with Dataview showing all daily notes on one page as if they were one continuous file (ersatz <span class="small-caps">OBTF</span>), check.</p>
<p>But half an hour in, I stopped. It didn’t matter how sweet or OBTF-like the setup was, something wasn’t feeling right. Dataview gave me a continuous view, but it doesn’t work outside of Obsidian. That’s not a big deal because the result is solely for convenience.</p>
<p>The problem lies with the possibilities that surface due to so many moving parts. I know what I’m like! A collection of daily notes one day, a wiki-linked system for navigating them the next. With <span class="small-caps">CSS</span> to make them look pretty. That is exactly the kind of<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>fun’ that has me spending more time on my system than in it.</p>
<p>And yet, I reserve the right to play in the daily notes — or any other — playground, with sensible boundaries. Because I enjoy the process, and learn a lot from the doing. Nothing wrong with that. But for daily capture of notes and ideas, shifting to one big text file had felt freeing two years ago and it still felt that way now.</p>
<p>Many people do well with daily notes; I find them a gateway to distraction and unnecessary complexity. The principle here is to do what feels best to you, no matter how enthusiastic others are about the alternatives.</p>
<h3 id="find-your-simple-hold-it-close">Find your simple, hold it close</h3>
<p>And so I am going to leave daily notes (in separate files) alone. At least for now. I’ll continue throwing every random thought and note into a single text file, for as long as it feels helpful to do so. Because that’s what keeps me from all-consuming rabbit-holes of distraction.</p>
<p>I’m not trying to recreate a digital bullet journal, and I’m no longer dividing groups of dated entries with Markdown headers. I have a set of basic tags for common themes, but other than that my text file is like a paper notebook.</p>
<p>When I want to find where I wrote that thing last week, I flick back through the pages and look for it. To do the same thing in a text file I scroll, or enter key words into the search bar.</p>
<p>Here’s what I’ve learned from my separate daily notes experiment.</p>
<ul>
<li>Daily notes aren’t the problem, and one big text file is not the solution. You have to tailor things to suit yourself, not me or anybody else.<br>
</li>
<li>A practice may make a lot of sense and be successfully used by many people, but if it distracts you or makes your life more complicated you will be better off without it.<br>
</li>
<li>A practice may appear<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>simple’ and still have the effect of complicating your life. Your system should be no simpler than is needed to get your work done.<br>
</li>
<li>You can try and dissect the whys and wherefores of the productivity practices that do and do not work for you, but you don’t need to do that before getting on with life. Don’t let an incomplete analysis stop you from getting on with your most important work.</li>
</ul>
<p>How did I stop Obsidian’s daily notes from messing with my head? By observing myself and acknowledging that separate files aren’t where I need to be right now.</p>
<p>Two roads diverged… and I took the one least likely to see me wandering from the path that leads to where I want to go.</p>
